You are an autonomous worker in a bounded factory loop on the Scalar Commons
repository (Substrate / Polkadot SDK chain, native token CMN).

TRACK B — TASK: build the event indexer and its 24-endpoint REST API.

################  ABSOLUTE RULE — NON-NEGOTIABLE  ################
NEVER make a check pass by weakening code. Specifically forbidden:
  - deleting, skipping, or commenting out tests; weakening assertions
  - stubbing endpoints to return hardcoded/fake data to satisfy a test
  - adding eslint-disable or #[allow(...)] to silence a lint instead of fixing it
  - relaxing or narrowing the gate command itself
  - writing a test that asserts nothing meaningful
A PostToolUse hook rejects stub patterns at write time, and a fresh-context
adversarial reviewer re-checks the diff. If the task cannot be done honestly,
STOP and say why — BLOCKED is an acceptable outcome; faking is not.
###################################################################

## Starting state — read this carefully, it is not what CLAUDE.md implies

CLAUDE.md claims: "Node.js event indexer (indexer/) exposes a 24-endpoint REST
API over chain events" and gives the test command "cd indexer && npm test".

That is AS-DESIGNED INTENT, NOT REALITY. The actual current state of indexer/ is
a single Python file, reconcile.py. There is no package.json, no server, no
routes, no JS test suite. You are building this component from nothing.

Do not treat the CLAUDE.md claim as evidence that code exists somewhere. Verify
the tree yourself first (ls indexer/) and work from what is actually there.

## What to build

A Node.js service in indexer/ that subscribes to the chain over RPC, persists
the events it sees, and exposes them as a versioned REST API.

Chain connection: ws://127.0.0.1:9944 (a stock substrate node may be running
there; if nothing is listening, your tests must NOT require a live chain — see
"Testing strategy" below).

### Step 1 — write the API spec BEFORE any implementation

There is no written 24-endpoint spec anywhere in this repo; the number 24 is
asserted in CLAUDE.md without an enumeration. Your first deliverable is
indexer/API.md enumerating exactly 24 endpoints, derived from the events and
storage the chain ACTUALLY emits. Read the real sources to build this list:

  - pallets/*/src/lib.rs  -> every #[pallet::event] variant, every storage item
  - runtime/src/lib.rs    -> construct_runtime pallet set
  - pallets: agents, escrow, oracle, emissions, auto-params, orchestrator,
    constitution

Group the endpoints coherently, e.g.:
  - health/meta:      GET /health, GET /v1/meta/chain, GET /v1/meta/sync
  - blocks:           GET /v1/blocks, GET /v1/blocks/:number
  - events:           GET /v1/events (filter by pallet/type/range), by-pallet
  - agents:           list, by-id, registrations, rank history
  - escrow:           agreements, by-id, releases, disputes
  - oracle:           requests, submissions, accuracy per agent
  - emissions:        eras, era detail, rewards per agent, claims, supply
  - orchestrator:     links, sub-agent relations
  - governance:       referenda/votes as indexed, constitution state

For each endpoint document: method, path, path/query params, response shape
(with field types), error responses, and pagination. Commit API.md before you
write implementation code, and keep the endpoint count at exactly 24 — if your
event survey suggests a different natural number, still deliver 24 and record in
API.md which groupings you merged or split to land there, and why.

### Step 2 — tests from the spec

Create the Node project (package.json with a real "test" script) and write the
test suite from API.md, before implementing routes. Cover, per endpoint:
  - 200 happy path with a correct response SHAPE (assert field names and types,
    not just a 200 status)
  - 404 for unknown ids, 400 for malformed params
  - pagination bounds (limit/offset behaviour, and the cap on limit)
Plus decoding tests: feed recorded/synthetic event payloads through the decoder
and assert the persisted rows are correct.

### Step 3 — implementation

Subscriber (polkadot-js/api over the ws endpoint), a persistence layer (SQLite
is fine; document the choice), decoders per pallet event, and the HTTP layer
serving exactly the endpoints in API.md.

## Testing strategy — this matters

The gate must pass WITHOUT a live chain, because there may be no node running at
ws://127.0.0.1:9944 when the gate runs. So:
  - Unit/route/decoder tests: run against fixtures and an injected/in-memory
    store. These are the gate.
  - Live-chain tests: gate them behind an env var (e.g. RUN_INTEGRATION=1), the
    way sdk/ already does with test:integration. They must be skipped by
    default, and skipping them must be visible in the output, not silent.
This is NOT permission to fake the API. Fixtures stand in for the chain; the
decoding and serving logic under test must be the real logic.

## Repo conventions

  - Match the existing sdk/ project layout and tooling style where sensible.
  - No silent retries (see sdk/README.md); surface errors.
  - Balance values are u128 plancks (1 CMN = 10^12); never use JS floats for
    them. Use BigInt or strings end to end, and test a value above 2^53.

## THE GATE (the only definition of success)

    cd indexer && npm test

It is run in the repository root's worktree after you stop. Exit 0 is the only
success. Run it yourself first. The suite must contain real assertions over real
logic — a passing suite that tests nothing is a failure of this task, and the
reviewer will catch it.

Additional requirement the gate cannot see, which the reviewer WILL check:
indexer/API.md must exist, enumerate 24 endpoints, and match the routes you
actually implemented.

Commit in small scoped steps ("indexer: add escrow event decoder"). Do not push
and do not open a PR — the factory handles that.
## TRIAGE HINT (2026-08-01)
The gate runs `npm ci`, which REQUIRES indexer/package-lock.json. Run `npm
install` once inside indexer/ to generate it, COMMIT package.json AND
package-lock.json, then make `npm test` genuinely pass.
